**Two Sum** asks: given an integer array `nums` and a `target`, return the indices of the two numbers that add up to `target`. The optimal approach scans the array once while storing each value's index in a hash map, so for each element we can check whether its complement (`target - nums[i]`) has already been seen.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public int[] TwoSum(int[] nums, int target)
    {
        var seen = new Dictionary<int, int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            long complement = (long)target - nums[i];
            if (seen.TryGetValue((int)complement, out int j))
                return new[] { j, i };
            seen[nums[i]] = i;
        }
        return System.Array.Empty<int>();
    }
}
```

Time: O(n). Space: O(n).

The `long` cast avoids overflow when `target - nums[i]` falls outside the `int` range.

